{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794558654235,"sessionID":"ses_e2bf86ad3ffe7OMdIoF41pRkUC","part":{"partID":"prt_1d407f3820027wpx1e97Ns96ai","sessionID":"ses_e2bf86ad3ffe7OMdIoF41pRkUC","messageID":"msg_1d407bf58001CeiGR9VWVkUmft","type":"tool","id":"call_f20b5d895f9c453bbf918f5f","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md, lines 1-280\n1: ---\n2: title: system-skill-advisor\n3: description: Picks the right skill for a non-trivial prompt with a calibrated score and an explicit trust state, so a runtime can route work without guessing and without leaking prompt text into logs.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"skill advisor\"\n6:   - \"gate 2 routing\"\n7:   - \"advisor_recommend\"\n8:   - \"which skill should handle this\"\n9:   - \"route this request\"\n10: version: 0.12.17.0\n11: ---\n12: \n13: # system-skill-advisor\n14: \n15: > Pick the right skill for any non-trivial prompt with a calibrated score and an explicit trust state, safe to call from hooks and safe to log.\n16: \n17: ---\n18: \n19: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n20: \n21: ### At a Glance\n22: \n23: | Aspect | What you get |\n24: |---|---|\n25: | **Use it for** | Deciding which skill should handle a non-trivial prompt with calibrated confidence |\n26: | **Invoke with** | \"which skill should handle this\", \"route this request\", \"advisor_recommend\", automatic Gate 2 routing |\n27: | **Works on** | Non-trivial prompts, scored against indexed skill metadata and a SQLite skill graph |\n28: | **Produces** | Ranked skill recommendations with per-lane attribution, a trust state and prompt-safe output |\n29: \n30: ### Purpose\n31: \n32: When a runtime meets an ambiguous prompt, guessing which skill to dispatch silently misroutes work. Guessing also hides the reason, so a bad route cannot be explained or repaired. And hook code that echoes the user prompt into logs creates a privacy problem that scales with every invocation.\n33: \n34: The advisor answers the \"which skill\" question with a calibrated score and an explicit trust state. It redacts raw prompt content from every response, so callers can log routing telemetry without logging what the user asked.\n35: \n36: ### When to Use\n37: \n38: Reach for the advisor when a runtime needs to pick a skill for a non-trivial prompt and guessing is not safe. The advisor answers the question and gets out of the way. It does not own the work the target skill performs.\n39: \n40: ### What It Does\n41: \n42: system-skill-advisor is the standalone Gate 2 routing surface for Spec Kit. It runs as a resident daemon behind one CLI front door, so routing can be tuned and rolled back without touching memory or code-graph systems. It fuses five scoring lanes into one calibrated recommendation, returns per-lane attribution without leaking prompt content and surfaces a trust state on every response so the caller knows whether to use the result or treat it as degraded.\n43: \n44: The single front door is the daemon-backed CLI at `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs`, which speaks the advisor's own newline-delimited protocol over a unix socket. When the daemon is unreachable, the CLI answers from the Python scorer at `runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py` and marks the response degraded, so the prompt-time brief reports `Advisor: stale` rather than claiming live. The skill also owns the documentation for the OpenCode plugin surface and the `/goal` plugin.\n45: \n46: ### The Five-Lane Scorer\n47: \n48: | Lane | Live Weight | What the skill knows how to operate |\n49: |---|---|---|\n50: | `explicit_author` | 0.42 | Author-declared skill ids and trigger metadata |\n51: | `lexical` | 0.28 | Text overlap and lexical evidence |\n52: | `graph_causal` | 0.13 | Skill graph relationships, including `depends_on` and `enhances` edges |\n53: | `derived_generated` | 0.12 | Sanitized derived metadata from prior runs |\n54: | `semantic_shadow` | 0.05 | Semantic embedding evidence, lowest fusion weight |\n55: \n56: ---\n57: \n58: ## 2. QUICK START\n59: \n60: **Step 1: Check advisor health.**\n61: \n62: ```bash\n63: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status --workspace-root \"$PWD\" --format json\n64: ```\n65: \n66: Expected result: a payload with `freshness`, `generation`, `trustState`, lane weights and `skillCount`. A `trustState` of `live` means the index is fresh and you can trust the next recommendation.\n67: \n68: **Step 2: Ask for a recommendation.**\n69: \n70: ```bash\n71: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"create a new agent\"}' --format json\n72: ```\n73: \n74: Expected result: a `recommendations[]` array of skill candidates ranked by score, with `freshness`, `trustState`, prompt-safe attribution metadata. Public responses never echo raw prompt content.\n75: \n76: **Step 3: Rebuild when status reports a non-live state.**\n77: \n78: ```bash\n79: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_rebuild --trusted --force true --format json\n80: ```\n81: \n82: Expected result: `rebuilt: true`, generation deltas, refreshed `skillCount`, diagnostics. Run when `advisor_status` reports a non-live or unavailable index.\n83: \n84: ### Gate 2 Caller Guidance\n85: \n86: Use the CLI as the Gate 2 path when no hook brief is present, when scripting a check, or when diagnosing hook behavior. The automatic brief and the CLI read the same daemon, so a manual check and the automatic route agree. `list-tools` enumerates all nine commands offline. Exit taxonomy: `0` success, `1` runtime error, `64` usage or schema error, `69` protocol or dist mismatch, `75` retryable daemon error.\n87: \n88: Two guardrails apply. First, callers bound the CLI call with their own timeout and fail open on expiry or exit `75`. The CLI starts the daemon when the socket is cold and answers from the local scorer when the daemon stays unreachable. A degraded answer is stale, not missing. Second, CLI calls are sent untrusted by default: the mutation commands `advisor_rebuild`, `skill_graph_scan`, apply-mode `skill_graph_propagate_enhances` require `--trusted` (or `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_CLI_TRUSTED=1`), which is the maintainer path. There is no MCP transport to fall back to.\n89: \n90: ```bash\n91: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status --workspace-root \"$PWD\" --format json\n92: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_rebuild --trusted --force true\n93: ```\n94: \n95: ### Runtime Environment Ownership\n96: \n97: `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` is owned by this hub because its live consumers are `runtime/lib/subprocess.ts` and `runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py`. Its default is `3000` ms. A timeout yields prompt-safe degraded context with a timeout marker. The system-spec-kit environment reference points here rather than defining a second contract.\n98: \n99: ### OpenCode Plugin Note\n100: \n101: The Skill Advisor bridge plugin injects routing advice at prompt time.\n102: \n103: - Constant advisor policy is delivered in full on the first proven message and after registered lifecycle boundaries.\n104: - Route-only repeats require confirmed primitive identity, valid transcript evidence, matching versioned state, stable generation/epoch clocks and an atomically advanced high-water mark.\n105: - When there is no brief, the plugin and the hooks emit a fallback that opens with one status line (`Advisor: outage (...)`, `Advisor: no skill matched.` or `Advisor: prompt skipped.`).\n106: - For setup-shaped prompts, no-match and outage fallbacks also name `/doctor:setup`.\n107: - A repeat in a confirmed session keeps that line and drops the directives.\n108: - With the opt-in `deduplicateTransforms` on, the plugin decides same-message transform dedup on the full block before lifecycle reduction, so a repeated transform for the same message is suppressed.\n109: - Full-delivery receipts commit after stdout handoff. A boundary failure poisons older receipts, and unsafe or unavailable durable state stays full.\n110: - `SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_DEDUP=0` restores always-full delivery.\n111: \n112: The separate `/goal` plugin:\n113: \n114: - Persists a session objective, injects a bounded active-goal block and exposes `opencode_goal` and `opencode_goal_status`.\n115: - Its command router delegates all state reads and writes to `opencode_goal` and `opencode_goal_status`.\n116: - Active continuation remains opt-in through `OPENCODE_GOAL_AUTONOMY`.\n117: - Live OpenCode-run tool invocation is verified: an `opencode serve` run lists `opencode_goal` and `opencode_goal_status` in the session tool set. A live model turn persists per-session state.\n118: \n119: ### Doctor Command Routing\n120: \n121: When no skill answers a setup or health prompt, the fallback names the runnable `/doctor:setup` line. When a skill and doctor bridge both pass without a typed `/doctor:` command, the skill stays first and the brief adds `Also: /doctor:<command> <target>` as its second line.\n122: \n123: ---\n124: \n125: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n126: \n127: ### The Fusion and the Options\n128: \n129: The advisor scores every prompt against the five lanes from Section 1, each producing its own evidence, then fuses them into one calibrated ranking. Public responses include per-lane contribution labels so hook telemetry stays safe to log without revealing the user's prompt text.\n130: \n131: The lane weights live in `runtime/lib/scorer/lane-registry.ts`. The scorer reads `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_LANE_WEIGHTS_JSON`. The launcher allowlist passes it through to the daemon child. An env change needs a daemon restart to apply. Use the env variable for experiments. Durable tuning is editing `lane-registry.ts` with measured evidence. Run `advisor_validate` before and after the change. Ship the diff with doc updates across the feature catalog and the advisor scorer reference.\n132: \n133: Deep-loop routing modes are projected from `mode-registry.json` into generated constants in `runtime/lib/scorer/aliases.ts` and the Python compatibility script. A SHA-256 projection hash is folded into the recommend cache signature and checked by the routing-registry drift guard. When a generated deep-loop alias resolves to the merged workflow skill, `advisor_recommend` publishes an optional `workflowMode` field for downstream routing.\n134: \n135: `advisor_recommend` accepts eight options: `topK` sets how many candidates to return (1 to 10), `includeAttribution` adds per-lane score breakdowns, `includeAbstainReasons` surfaces why lower-ranked candidates were not selected, `confidenceThreshold` overrides the minimum surfaced confidence, `uncertaintyThreshold` overrides the maximum surfaced uncertainty and `includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. Passing `false` skips the compiled-route enrichment so no compiled-route subprocess starts. The prompt hooks send `true`, because a recommendation's `command` comes from its compiled route. `compiledRouteTopOnly` is an optional boolean that defaults to false. Passing `true` looks up the compiled route for the first recommendation only, which saves one subprocess for each further hub. The prompt hooks send it as `true`, so only the top pick can carry a `command`. `surfaceHint` forwards a code surface the caller already knows, such as `SKILLED` (1 to 64 characters), to the compiled-route front door, which ignores an unknown one. `advisor_validate` requires `confirmHeavyRun: true` because it executes the full validation bundle (corpus, holdout, parity, safety, latency).\n136: \n137: ### Prompt-Safe Attribution\n138: \n139: Every public response strips raw prompt content. Attribution is per-lane only. The same safety contract applies to all runtime hooks and to the Python compatibility shim, so hook telemetry and script output stay safe to log at any scale.\n140: \n141: Shadow-delta JSONL recording is opt-in. `advisor_recommend` includes the prompt-safe `_shadow` comparison payload in its response, but it writes no durable shadow delta file unless `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_SHADOW_DELTA_PATH` is set or `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_SHADOW_DELTA_ENABLED=1` or `true` enables the default sink. Both env names are forwarded by the advisor launcher allowlist.\n142: \n143: ### Freshness and the Trust Contract\n144: \n145: A daemon watches every `SKILL.md`, `ROUTER.md` and `graph-metadata.json` under `.skilled/skills/`. When a watched source changes, the daemon schedules an incremental reindex, publishes a fresh generation after the rebuild and invalidates the recommendation cache. It holds a single-writer lease. Explicit trusted mutation paths are `advisor_rebuild`, `skill_graph_scan` and real `skill_graph_propagate_enhances` apply writes (`mode: apply` with `dryRun !== true`). Corrupt-database recovery may also move aside and recreate the database during lazy initialization.\n146: \n147: Every response carries a trust state so the caller knows what to do next.\n148: \n149: | Trust State | Meaning | Caller Action |\n150: |---|---|---|\n151: | `live` | Index is fresh and queryable | Use the recommendation directly |\n152: | `stale` | Index is queryable but a source changed since the last build | Use scored recommendations with a caveat, then call `advisor_rebuild` |\n153: | `absent` | The SQLite database is missing | Call `advisor_rebuild`. Do not act on an empty result |\n154: | `unavailable` | The subsystem cannot be reached | The CLI answers from the local scorer and marks the result degraded. Treat it as stale, then rebuild |\n155: \n156: ### The SQLite Skill Graph\n157: \n158: The advisor ships a package-local SQLite database that stores cross-skill edges extracted from every skill's `graph-metadata.json`. The graph supports ten query types through `skill_graph_query`: `depends_on`, `dependents`, `enhances`, `enhanced_by`, `family_members`, `conflicts`, `transitive_path`, `hub_skills`, `orphans`, `subgraph`. `skill_graph_propagate_enhances` detects, proposes or applies missing inbound `enhances` declarations across skills. Report, propose, dry-run apply are read-safe. Real apply writes require trusted caller context.\n159: \n160: ### Doc-Frontmatter Trigger Harvest\n161: \n162: With `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_DOC_TRIGGERS=true`, `skill_graph_scan` also harvests frontmatter (`title`, `description`, `trigger_phrases`, `importance_tier`, `contextType`) from every markdown file under each skill's `references/` and `assets/` (READMEs excluded) into a `skill_docs` table. The watcher registers those docs so edits re-index the owning skill. Doc phrases score inside the `derived_generated` lane: top-3 docs per skill, tier-weighted, contribution capped at 0.45, so they assist ranking but cannot hard-route alone. Matching recommendations carry an optional `matchedDocs` field (max 3 sanitized skill-relative paths) pointing at the exact doc to open.\n163: \n164: Flag unset (the default) changes nothing: no harvest, no watch targets, identical scoring. Deployment note: the launcher forwards only allowlisted env to the daemon child, so the flag works because it sits in `CHILD_ENV_ALLOWLIST` in `system-skill-advisor-launcher.cjs`. Any future advisor env flag needs the same entry.\n165: \n166: ### Private Local Skills\n167: \n168: A skill whose folder name starts with `local-` belongs to one checkout. It is usually a gitignored symlink under `.skilled/skills/` that points at a folder in another repository. The local advisor scans include it, so it can route, and every tracked-output writer skips it by name before its first probe, so it never reaches the fallback graph, a CI gate or a doctor finding.\n169: \n170: A scan follows a top-level `local-*` link only when the link's real path resolves to a directory inside the skills root or inside a root named in `SPECKIT_PRIVATE_SKILL_ROOTS`. That variable holds absolute paths joined by the platform path delimiter, which is `:` on macOS and Linux. A link to any other place is skipped. `runtime/lib/private-skill-target.cjs` holds this containment rule, `runtime/lib/private-skill-target.ts` wraps it with types, and `runtime/scripts/skill_advisor_runtime.py` holds its Python mirror. The variable sits in `CHILD_ENV_ALLOWLIST`, so the launcher forwards it to the daemon child. Every advisor process needs it for such a link, so a daemon started without it skips every private skill whose link points outside the skills root.\n171: \n172: ### The Embedder\n173: \n174: The `semantic_shadow` lane runs against the pluggable embedder layer in `@spec-kit/shared/embeddings/`, which the advisor owns. The registry holds text-tuned embedding models and defaults through a local-first cascade to a local model when no embedder is explicitly set. For the full model registry, the cascade tier table and the swap workflow, see `INSTALL-GUIDE.md` §12.\n175: \n176: Command rows use the same embedder. The bridge deriver writes an `embeddingText` for each of the 45 command rows the TypeScript scorer serves, and `skill_graph_scan` embeds it into its own `vec_cmd_<dim>` table beside the skill vectors. The scan skips a row whose text has not changed and prunes a row that is no longer generated. A command row normally passes only on one of its own phrases or its slash string. It can also pass on meaning: the prompt's real embedding has to reach a cosine of 0.72 against the row and lead the next command row by 0.02. A command that passed this way ranks first only when it leads the top passing skill by the same 0.02. A prompt that asks for no change, such as \"no edits\" or \"just list\", rules out every command on meaning. A read-only question lets only a command whose route only reads pass on meaning. Without the local embedder (Ollama serving `nomic-embed-text`) no cosine exists, so command rows pass on their phrases alone.\n177: \n178: ### The Nine Commands\n179: \n180: The CLI exposes nine commands, each accepting snake case, kebab case and camel case spellings. `list-tools` enumerates all nine. `skill_graph_propagate_enhances` is only trust-gated for the mutating apply path. Report, propose, dry-run apply stay read-safe. You have already met the first three in Quick Start.\n181: \n182: | Command | Purpose |\n183: |---|---|\n184: | `advisor_recommend` | Recommend skills for a prompt (`topK`, `includeAttribution`, `includeAbstainReasons`, `confidenceThreshold`, `uncertaintyThreshold`, `includeCompiledRoute`, `compiledRouteTopOnly`, `surfaceHint`) |\n185: | `advisor_rebuild` | Rebuild the advisor index from checked-in metadata |\n186: | `advisor_status` | Report freshness, generation, lane weights, skill count, scan times and a trust state that reads `unavailable` when the daemon is down |\n187: | `advisor_validate` | Run the corpus, holdout, parity, safety and latency bundle (`confirmHeavyRun: true` required) |\n188: | `skill_graph_scan` | Index every `graph-metadata.json` into the SQLite skill graph |\n189: | `skill_graph_query` | Traverse the skill graph (the ten query types above) |\n190: | `skill_graph_status` | Report graph health, counts and staleness |\n191: | `skill_graph_validate` | Validate schema drift, broken edges, dependency cycles, weight bands, reciprocal symmetry, orphan skills and derived-freshness warnings |\n192: | `skill_graph_propagate_enhances` | Propose or apply missing inbound `enhances` edges. Apply writes are trusted-only |\n193: \n194: ---\n195: \n196: ## 4. INTEGRATION & NAVIGATION\n197: \n198: ### Related Skills\n199: \n200: | Skill | Relationship |\n201: |---|---|\n202: | `system-spec-kit` | Owns memory, spec folders and continuity. The advisor depends on it for the shared embeddings stack and the IPC bridge. All other code is isolated |\n203: | `sk-code`, `sk-doc`, `sk-git`, `mcp-code-mode` | Target skills the advisor recommends. Once the advisor points at one, that skill owns the work |\n204: \n205: Skill-root metadata ownership follows the [canonical contract](../sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/shared/skill-root-metadata-contract.md). The advisor identity input is `graph-metadata.json`, not `description.json`.\n206: \n207: ---\n208: \n209: ## 5. TROUBLESHOOTING\n210: \n211: | What you see | Why | Fix |\n212: |---|---|---|\n213: | `trustState: \"absent\"` | The advisor SQLite database is missing or empty | Call `advisor_rebuild`. If that fails, check `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DB_DIR` and disk permissions |\n214: | `trustState: \"unavailable\"` | The daemon cannot be reached | The CLI already answers from the local scorer and marks the result degraded. Rebuild with `advisor_rebuild` when the daemon is back |\n215: | Top-2 candidates within 0.1 of each other | Ambiguous prompt. Two skills are equally plausible | Surface both candidates instead of routing silently |\n216: | `advisor_validate` reports outside the dated bounded-delta gate | Scorer behavior changed or fixtures drifted | Inspect `perSkill[]`, `slices.corpus`, [`validation-baselines.md`](./references/scoring/validation-baselines.md) |\n217: | Recommendations omit a newly-added skill | The daemon has not observed the new file yet | Call `advisor_rebuild` or wait for the watcher to fire |\n218: | CLI reports a mutation `requires --trusted` (exit 64) | The trusted-mutation gate fails closed on untrusted calls | Re-run with `--trusted` or set `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_CLI_TRUSTED=1` if you are the maintainer |\n219: | CLI exits 69 with a stale-build message | The dist build is older than the sources | Rebuild with `npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build`, then rerun |\n220: | Routing feels off, or you are not sure the graph is current | Lane weights, graph freshness, router reach, skill budgets or a parent hub may have drifted | Run `/doctor:skill-advisor`. `tune` re-tunes the scoring lanes, `rebuild` rebuilds `skill-graph.sqlite` with a backup, and `skill-graph-freshness`, `router-reach`, `skill-budget` and `parent-skill` are read-only audits |\n221: \n222: ---\n223: \n224: ## 6. FAQ\n225: \n226: **Q: Why is the advisor a separate daemon?**\n227: \n228: A: Routing is operationally distinct from memory. You can roll back, restart or tune the advisor without touching memory state. One daemon with one CLI front door keeps that boundary safe.\n229: \n230: **Q: Can I change the lane weights?**\n231: \n232: A: Yes, with measured evidence. `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_LANE_WEIGHTS_JSON` is allowlisted through the launcher for experiments (daemon restart required). The durable path is a source edit in `runtime/lib/scorer/lane-registry.ts`: run `advisor_validate` to capture a baseline, change the weights, re-run validate, ship the diff with doc updates in `references/scoring/advisor-scorer.md` and the feature catalog.\n233: \n234: **Q: How does the advisor stay safe to call from hooks?**\n235: \n236: A: Responses redact raw prompt content. Attribution is per-lane only. Every runtime hook and the Python compatibility shim follow the same redaction contract, so telemetry stays safe at any call volume.\n237: \n238: **Q: What is the relationship to system-spec-kit?**\n239: \n240: A: Memory, spec folders and continuity stay in `system-spec-kit`. The advisor depends on it for the shared embeddings stack and the IPC bridge. All other code is isolated.\n241: \n242: **Q: Where are the runtime hooks documented?**\n243: \n244: A: `hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md` covers the prompt-time hook contract across every runtime (Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin, Pi) and the OpenCode plugin. The advisor's own handler, the Pi extension and the CLI fallback live under `hooks/`. Claude, Codex, Cursor and Devin enter through thin shims in `system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/<runtime>/`, and the OpenCode plugin is `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js`.\n245: \n246: ---\n247: \n248: ## 7. VERIFICATION\n249: \n250: | Check | How to run it |\n251: |---|---|\n252: | README structure | `python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md --type readme` reports zero issues |\n253: | TypeScript build | `npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run typecheck && npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build` exits 0 |\n254: | Playbook | Run the manual testing playbook scenarios under `manual-testing-playbook/` in a live session |\n255: | Validation battery | `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_validate --json '{\"confirmHeavyRun\":true}' --format json` reports within the dated bounded-delta gate in [`validation-baselines.md`](./references/scoring/validation-baselines.md) |\n256: \n257: ---\n258: \n259: ## 8. RELATED DOCUMENTS\n260: \n261: | Document | Purpose |\n262: |---|---|\n263: | [`SKILL.md`](./SKILL.md) | Runtime routing instructions and invariants |\n264: | [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) | Machine router: intent map, resource map and loading levels |\n265: | [`ARCHITECTURE.md`](./ARCHITECTURE.md) | System design, CLI front door, data flow and database layout |\n266: | [`INSTALL-GUIDE.md`](./INSTALL-GUIDE.md) | Setup, runtime hooks, rollback and embedder selection |\n267: | [`references/runtime/tool-ids-reference.md`](./references/runtime/tool-ids-reference.md) | All nine tool ids with inputs and output shapes |\n268: | [`references/scoring/advisor-scorer.md`](./references/scoring/advisor-scorer.md) | Lane attribution model and fusion rules |\n269: | [`references/scoring/lane-weight-tuning.md`](./references/scoring/lane-weight-tuning.md) | Evidence requirements for lane weight changes |\n270: | [`references/scoring/validation-baselines.md`](./references/scoring/validation-baselines.md) | Validation baselines and troubleshooting |\n271: | [`references/runtime/freshness-contract.md`](./references/runtime/freshness-contract.md) | Trust-state vocabulary and caller obligations |\n272: | [`references/runtime/daemon-lease-contract.md`](./references/runtime/daemon-lease-contract.md) | Single-writer daemon lease semantics |\n273: | [`references/runtime/standalone-mcp-shape.md`](./references/runtime/standalone-mcp-shape.md) | ADR-001 standalone topology record (historical) |\n274: | [`references/graph/skill-graph-query-cookbook.md`](./references/graph/skill-graph-query-cookbook.md) | Worked examples for all query types |\n275: | [`references/graph/skill-graph-drift.md`](./references/graph/skill-graph-drift.md) | Detect and reconcile SQLite drift from source files |\n276: | [`hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md`](./hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md) | Prompt-time hook contract across runtimes |\n277: | [`feature-catalog/feature-catalog.md`](./feature-catalog/feature-catalog.md) | Current feature inventory |\n278: | [`manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md`](./manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md) | Manual validation scenario index |\n279: | [`feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/goal-opencode-plugin.md`](./feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/goal-opencode-plugin.md) | `/goal` OpenCode plugin reference |\n280: | [`changelog/`](./changelog/) | Versioned changelogs |","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md, lines 1-280\n1: ---\n2: title: system-skill-advisor\n3: description: Picks the right skill for a non-trivial prompt with a calibrated score and an explicit trust state, so a runtime can route work without guessing and without leaking prompt text into logs.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"skill advisor\"\n6:   - \"gate 2 routing\"\n7:   - \"advisor_recommend\"\n8:   - \"which skill should handle this\"\n9:   - \"route this request\"\n10: version: 0.12.17.0\n11: ---\n12: \n13: # system-skill-advisor\n14: \n15: > Pick the right skill for any non-trivial prompt with a calibrated score and an explicit trust state, safe to call from hooks and safe to log.\n16: \n17: ---\n18: \n19: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n20: \n21: ### At a Glance\n22: \n23: | Aspect | What you get |\n24: |---|---|\n25: | **Use it for** | Deciding which skill should handle a non-trivial prompt with calibrated confidence |\n26: | **Invoke with** | \"which skill should handle this\", \"route this request\", \"advisor_recommend\", automatic Gate 2 routing |\n27: | **Works on** | Non-trivial prompts, scored against indexed skill metadata and a SQLite skill graph |\n28: | **Produces** | Ranked skill recommendations with per-lane attribution, a trust state and prompt-safe output |\n29: \n30: ### Purpose\n31: \n32: When a runtime meets an ambiguous prompt, guessing which skill to dispatch silently misroutes work. Guessing also hides the reason, so a bad route cannot be explained or repaired. And hook code that echoes the user prompt into logs creates a privacy problem that scales with every invocation.\n33: \n34: The advisor answers the \"which skill\" question with a calibrated score and an explicit trust state. It redacts raw prompt content from every response, so callers can log routing telemetry without logging what the user asked.\n35: \n36: ### When to Use\n37: \n38: Reach for the advisor when a runtime needs to pick a skill for a non-trivial prompt and guessing is not safe. The advisor answers the question and gets out of the way. It does not own the work the target skill performs.\n39: \n40: ### What It Does\n41: \n42: system-skill-advisor is the standalone Gate 2 routing surface for Spec Kit. It runs as a resident daemon behind one CLI front door, so routing can be tuned and rolled back without touching memory or code-graph systems. It fuses five scoring lanes into one calibrated recommendation, returns per-lane attribution without leaking prompt content and surfaces a trust state on every response so the caller knows whether to use the result or treat it as degraded.\n43: \n44: The single front door is the daemon-backed CLI at `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs`, which speaks the advisor's own newline-delimited protocol over a unix socket. When the daemon is unreachable, the CLI answers from the Python scorer at `runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py` and marks the response degraded, so the prompt-time brief reports `Advisor: stale` rather than claiming live. The skill also owns the documentation for the OpenCode plugin surface and the `/goal` plugin.\n45: \n46: ### The Five-Lane Scorer\n47: \n48: | Lane | Live Weight | What the skill knows how to operate |\n49: |---|---|---|\n50: | `explicit_author` | 0.42 | Author-declared skill ids and trigger metadata |\n51: | `lexical` | 0.28 | Text overlap and lexical evidence |\n52: | `graph_causal` | 0.13 | Skill graph relationships, including `depends_on` and `enhances` edges |\n53: | `derived_generated` | 0.12 | Sanitized derived metadata from prior runs |\n54: | `semantic_shadow` | 0.05 | Semantic embedding evidence, lowest fusion weight |\n55: \n56: ---\n57: \n58: ## 2. QUICK START\n59: \n60: **Step 1: Check advisor health.**\n61: \n62: ```bash\n63: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status --workspace-root \"$PWD\" --format json\n64: ```\n65: \n66: Expected result: a payload with `freshness`, `generation`, `trustState`, lane weights and `skillCount`. A `trustState` of `live` means the index is fresh and you can trust the next recommendation.\n67: \n68: **Step 2: Ask for a recommendation.**\n69: \n70: ```bash\n71: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"create a new agent\"}' --format json\n72: ```\n73: \n74: Expected result: a `recommendations[]` array of skill candidates ranked by score, with `freshness`, `trustState`, prompt-safe attribution metadata. Public responses never echo raw prompt content.\n75: \n76: **Step 3: Rebuild when status reports a non-live state.**\n77: \n78: ```bash\n79: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_rebuild --trusted --force true --format json\n80: ```\n81: \n82: Expected result: `rebuilt: true`, generation deltas, refreshed `skillCount`, diagnostics. Run when `advisor_status` reports a non-live or unavailable index.\n83: \n84: ### Gate 2 Caller Guidance\n85: \n86: Use the CLI as the Gate 2 path when no hook brief is present, when scripting a check, or when diagnosing hook behavior. The automatic brief and the CLI read the same daemon, so a manual check and the automatic route agree. `list-tools` enumerates all nine commands offline. Exit taxonomy: `0` success, `1` runtime error, `64` usage or schema error, `69` protocol or dist mismatch, `75` retryable daemon error.\n87: \n88: Two guardrails apply. First, callers bound the CLI call with their own timeout and fail open on expiry or exit `75`. The CLI starts the daemon when the socket is cold and answers from the local scorer when the daemon stays unreachable. A degraded answer is stale, not missing. Second, CLI calls are sent untrusted by default: the mutation commands `advisor_rebuild`, `skill_graph_scan`, apply-mode `skill_graph_propagate_enhances` require `--trusted` (or `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_CLI_TRUSTED=1`), which is the maintainer path. There is no MCP transport to fall back to.\n89: \n90: ```bash\n91: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status --workspace-root \"$PWD\" --format json\n92: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_rebuild --trusted --force true\n93: ```\n94: \n95: ### Runtime Environment Ownership\n96: \n97: `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` is owned by this hub because its live consumers are `runtime/lib/subprocess.ts` and `runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py`. Its default is `3000` ms. A timeout yields prompt-safe degraded context with a timeout marker. The system-spec-kit environment reference points here rather than defining a second contract.\n98: \n99: ### OpenCode Plugin Note\n100: \n101: The Skill Advisor bridge plugin injects routing advice at prompt time.\n102: \n103: - Constant advisor policy is delivered in full on the first proven message and after registered lifecycle boundaries.\n104: - Route-only repeats require confirmed primitive identity, valid transcript evidence, matching versioned state, stable generation/epoch clocks and an atomically advanced high-water mark.\n105: - When there is no brief, the plugin and the hooks emit a fallback that opens with one status line (`Advisor: outage (...)`, `Advisor: no skill matched.` or `Advisor: prompt skipped.`).\n106: - For setup-shaped prompts, no-match and outage fallbacks also name `/doctor:setup`.\n107: - A repeat in a confirmed session keeps that line and drops the directives.\n108: - With the opt-in `deduplicateTransforms` on, the plugin decides same-message transform dedup on the full block before lifecycle reduction, so a repeated transform for the same message is suppressed.\n109: - Full-delivery receipts commit after stdout handoff. A boundary failure poisons older receipts, and unsafe or unavailable durable state stays full.\n110: - `SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_DEDUP=0` restores always-full delivery.\n111: \n112: The separate `/goal` plugin:\n113: \n114: - Persists a session objective, injects a bounded active-goal block and exposes `opencode_goal` and `opencode_goal_status`.\n115: - Its command router delegates all state reads and writes to `opencode_goal` and `opencode_goal_status`.\n116: - Active continuation remains opt-in through `OPENCODE_GOAL_AUTONOMY`.\n117: - Live OpenCode-run tool invocation is verified: an `opencode serve` run lists `opencode_goal` and `opencode_goal_status` in the session tool set. A live model turn persists per-session state.\n118: \n119: ### Doctor Command Routing\n120: \n121: When no skill answers a setup or health prompt, the fallback names the runnable `/doctor:setup` line. When a skill and doctor bridge both pass without a typed `/doctor:` command, the skill stays first and the brief adds `Also: /doctor:<command> <target>` as its second line.\n122: \n123: ---\n124: \n125: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n126: \n127: ### The Fusion and the Options\n128: \n129: The advisor scores every prompt against the five lanes from Section 1, each producing its own evidence, then fuses them into one calibrated ranking. Public responses include per-lane contribution labels so hook telemetry stays safe to log without revealing the user's prompt text.\n130: \n131: The lane weights live in `runtime/lib/scorer/lane-registry.ts`. The scorer reads `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_LANE_WEIGHTS_JSON`. The launcher allowlist passes it through to the daemon child. An env change needs a daemon restart to apply. Use the env variable for experiments. Durable tuning is editing `lane-registry.ts` with measured evidence. Run `advisor_validate` before and after the change. Ship the diff with doc updates across the feature catalog and the advisor scorer reference.\n132: \n133: Deep-loop routing modes are projected from `mode-registry.json` into generated constants in `runtime/lib/scorer/aliases.ts` and the Python compatibility script. A SHA-256 projection hash is folded into the recommend cache signature and checked by the routing-registry drift guard. When a generated deep-loop alias resolves to the merged workflow skill, `advisor_recommend` publishes an optional `workflowMode` field for downstream routing.\n134: \n135: `advisor_recommend` accepts eight options: `topK` sets how many candidates to return (1 to 10), `includeAttribution` adds per-lane score breakdowns, `includeAbstainReasons` surfaces why lower-ranked candidates were not selected, `confidenceThreshold` overrides the minimum surfaced confidence, `uncertaintyThreshold` overrides the maximum surfaced uncertainty and `includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. Passing `false` skips the compiled-route enrichment so no compiled-route subprocess starts. The prompt hooks send `true`, because a recommendation's `command` comes from its compiled route. `compiledRouteTopOnly` is an optional boolean that defaults to false. Passing `true` looks up the compiled route for the first recommendation only, which saves one subprocess for each further hub. The prompt hooks send it as `true`, so only the top pick can carry a `command`. `surfaceHint` forwards a code surface the caller already knows, such as `SKILLED` (1 to 64 characters), to the compiled-route front door, which ignores an unknown one. `advisor_validate` requires `confirmHeavyRun: true` because it executes the full validation bundle (corpus, holdout, parity, safety, latency).\n136: \n137: ### Prompt-Safe Attribution\n138: \n139: Every public response strips raw prompt content. Attribution is per-lane only. The same safety contract applies to all runtime hooks and to the Python compatibility shim, so hook telemetry and script output stay safe to log at any scale.\n140: \n141: Shadow-delta JSONL recording is opt-in. `advisor_recommend` includes the prompt-safe `_shadow` comparison payload in its response, but it writes no durable shadow delta file unless `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_SHADOW_DELTA_PATH` is set or `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_SHADOW_DELTA_ENABLED=1` or `true` enables the default sink. Both env names are forwarded by the advisor launcher allowlist.\n142: \n143: ### Freshness and the Trust Contract\n144: \n145: A daemon watches every `SKILL.md`, `ROUTER.md` and `graph-metadata.json` under `.skilled/skills/`. When a watched source changes, the daemon schedules an incremental reindex, publishes a fresh generation after the rebuild and invalidates the recommendation cache. It holds a single-writer lease. Explicit trusted mutation paths are `advisor_rebuild`, `skill_graph_scan` and real `skill_graph_propagate_enhances` apply writes (`mode: apply` with `dryRun !== true`). Corrupt-database recovery may also move aside and recreate the database during lazy initialization.\n146: \n147: Every response carries a trust state so the caller knows what to do next.\n148: \n149: | Trust State | Meaning | Caller Action |\n150: |---|---|---|\n151: | `live` | Index is fresh and queryable | Use the recommendation directly |\n152: | `stale` | Index is queryable but a source changed since the last build | Use scored recommendations with a caveat, then call `advisor_rebuild` |\n153: | `absent` | The SQLite database is missing | Call `advisor_rebuild`. Do not act on an empty result |\n154: | `unavailable` | The subsystem cannot be reached | The CLI answers from the local scorer and marks the result degraded. Treat it as stale, then rebuild |\n155: \n156: ### The SQLite Skill Graph\n157: \n158: The advisor ships a package-local SQLite database that stores cross-skill edges extracted from every skill's `graph-metadata.json`. The graph supports ten query types through `skill_graph_query`: `depends_on`, `dependents`, `enhances`, `enhanced_by`, `family_members`, `conflicts`, `transitive_path`, `hub_skills`, `orphans`, `subgraph`. `skill_graph_propagate_enhances` detects, proposes or applies missing inbound `enhances` declarations across skills. Report, propose, dry-run apply are read-safe. Real apply writes require trusted caller context.\n159: \n160: ### Doc-Frontmatter Trigger Harvest\n161: \n162: With `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_DOC_TRIGGERS=true`, `skill_graph_scan` also harvests frontmatter (`title`, `description`, `trigger_phrases`, `importance_tier`, `contextType`) from every markdown file under each skill's `references/` and `assets/` (READMEs excluded) into a `skill_docs` table. The watcher registers those docs so edits re-index the owning skill. Doc phrases score inside the `derived_generated` lane: top-3 docs per skill, tier-weighted, contribution capped at 0.45, so they assist ranking but cannot hard-route alone. Matching recommendations carry an optional `matchedDocs` field (max 3 sanitized skill-relative paths) pointing at the exact doc to open.\n163: \n164: Flag unset (the default) changes nothing: no harvest, no watch targets, identical scoring. Deployment note: the launcher forwards only allowlisted env to the daemon child, so the flag works because it sits in `CHILD_ENV_ALLOWLIST` in `system-skill-advisor-launcher.cjs`. Any future advisor env flag needs the same entry.\n165: \n166: ### Private Local Skills\n167: \n168: A skill whose folder name starts with `local-` belongs to one checkout. It is usually a gitignored symlink under `.skilled/skills/` that points at a folder in another repository. The local advisor scans include it, so it can route, and every tracked-output writer skips it by name before its first probe, so it never reaches the fallback graph, a CI gate or a doctor finding.\n169: \n170: A scan follows a top-level `local-*` link only when the link's real path resolves to a directory inside the skills root or inside a root named in `SPECKIT_PRIVATE_SKILL_ROOTS`. That variable holds absolute paths joined by the platform path delimiter, which is `:` on macOS and Linux. A link to any other place is skipped. `runtime/lib/private-skill-target.cjs` holds this containment rule, `runtime/lib/private-skill-target.ts` wraps it with types, and `runtime/scripts/skill_advisor_runtime.py` holds its Python mirror. The variable sits in `CHILD_ENV_ALLOWLIST`, so the launcher forwards it to the daemon child. Every advisor process needs it for such a link, so a daemon started without it skips every private skill whose link points outside the skills root.\n171: \n172: ### The Embedder\n173: \n174: The `semantic_shadow` lane runs against the pluggable embedder layer in `@spec-kit/shared/embeddings/`, which the advisor owns. The registry holds text-tuned embedding models and defaults through a local-first cascade to a local model when no embedder is explicitly set. For the full model registry, the cascade tier table and the swap workflow, see `INSTALL-GUIDE.md` §12.\n175: \n176: Command rows use the same embedder. The bridge deriver writes an `embeddingText` for each of the 45 command rows the TypeScript scorer serves, and `skill_graph_scan` embeds it into its own `vec_cmd_<dim>` table beside the skill vectors. The scan skips a row whose text has not changed and prunes a row that is no longer generated. A command row normally passes only on one of its own phrases or its slash string. It can also pass on meaning: the prompt's real embedding has to reach a cosine of 0.72 against the row and lead the next command row by 0.02. A command that passed this way ranks first only when it leads the top passing skill by the same 0.02. A prompt that asks for no change, such as \"no edits\" or \"just list\", rules out every command on meaning. A read-only question lets only a command whose route only reads pass on meaning. Without the local embedder (Ollama serving `nomic-embed-text`) no cosine exists, so command rows pass on their phrases alone.\n177: \n178: ### The Nine Commands\n179: \n180: The CLI exposes nine commands, each accepting snake case, kebab case and camel case spellings. `list-tools` enumerates all nine. `skill_graph_propagate_enhances` is only trust-gated for the mutating apply path. Report, propose, dry-run apply stay read-safe. You have already met the first three in Quick Start.\n181: \n182: | Command | Purpose |\n183: |---|---|\n184: | `advisor_recommend` | Recommend skills for a prompt (`topK`, `includeAttribution`, `includeAbstainReasons`, `confidenceThreshold`, `uncertaintyThreshold`, `includeCompiledRoute`, `compiledRouteTopOnly`, `surfaceHint`) |\n185: | `advisor_rebuild` | Rebuild the advisor index from checked-in metadata |\n186: | `advisor_status` | Report freshness, generation, lane weights, skill count, scan times and a trust state that reads `unavailable` when the daemon is down |\n187: | `advisor_validate` | Run the corpus, holdout, parity, safety and latency bundle (`confirmHeavyRun: true` required) |\n188: | `skill_graph_scan` | Index every `graph-metadata.json` into the SQLite skill graph |\n189: | `skill_graph_query` | Traverse the skill graph (the ten query types above) |\n190: | `skill_graph_status` | Report graph health, counts and staleness |\n191: | `skill_graph_validate` | Validate schema drift, broken edges, dependency cycles, weight bands, reciprocal symmetry, orphan skills and derived-freshness warnings |\n192: | `skill_graph_propagate_enhances` | Propose or apply missing inbound `enhances` edges. Apply writes are trusted-only |\n193: \n194: ---\n195: \n196: ## 4. INTEGRATION & NAVIGATION\n197: \n198: ### Related Skills\n199: \n200: | Skill | Relationship |\n201: |---|---|\n202: | `system-spec-kit` | Owns memory, spec folders and continuity. The advisor depends on it for the shared embeddings stack and the IPC bridge. All other code is isolated |\n203: | `sk-code`, `sk-doc`, `sk-git`, `mcp-code-mode` | Target skills the advisor recommends. Once the advisor points at one, that skill owns the work |\n204: \n205: Skill-root metadata ownership follows the [canonical contract](../sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/shared/skill-root-metadata-contract.md). The advisor identity input is `graph-metadata.json`, not `description.json`.\n206: \n207: ---\n208: \n209: ## 5. TROUBLESHOOTING\n210: \n211: | What you see | Why | Fix |\n212: |---|---|---|\n213: | `trustState: \"absent\"` | The advisor SQLite database is missing or empty | Call `advisor_rebuild`. If that fails, check `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DB_DIR` and disk permissions |\n214: | `trustState: \"unavailable\"` | The daemon cannot be reached | The CLI already answers from the local scorer and marks the result degraded. Rebuild with `advisor_rebuild` when the daemon is back |\n215: | Top-2 candidates within 0.1 of each other | Ambiguous prompt. Two skills are equally plausible | Surface both candidates instead of routing silently |\n216: | `advisor_validate` reports outside the dated bounded-delta gate | Scorer behavior changed or fixtures drifted | Inspect `perSkill[]`, `slices.corpus`, [`validation-baselines.md`](./references/scoring/validation-baselines.md) |\n217: | Recommendations omit a newly-added skill | The daemon has not observed the new file yet | Call `advisor_rebuild` or wait for the watcher to fire |\n218: | CLI reports a mutation `requires --trusted` (exit 64) | The trusted-mutation gate fails closed on untrusted calls | Re-run with `--trusted` or set `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_CLI_TRUSTED=1` if you are the maintainer |\n219: | CLI exits 69 with a stale-build message | The dist build is older than the sources | Rebuild with `npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build`, then rerun |\n220: | Routing feels off, or you are not sure the graph is current | Lane weights, graph freshness, router reach, skill budgets or a parent hub may have drifted | Run `/doctor:skill-advisor`. `tune` re-tunes the scoring lanes, `rebuild` rebuilds `skill-graph.sqlite` with a backup, and `skill-graph-freshness`, `router-reach`, `skill-budget` and `parent-skill` are read-only audits |\n221: \n222: ---\n223: \n224: ## 6. FAQ\n225: \n226: **Q: Why is the advisor a separate daemon?**\n227: \n228: A: Routing is operationally distinct from memory. You can roll back, restart or tune the advisor without touching memory state. One daemon with one CLI front door keeps that boundary safe.\n229: \n230: **Q: Can I change the lane weights?**\n231: \n232: A: Yes, with measured evidence. `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_LANE_WEIGHTS_JSON` is allowlisted through the launcher for experiments (daemon restart required). The durable path is a source edit in `runtime/lib/scorer/lane-registry.ts`: run `advisor_validate` to capture a baseline, change the weights, re-run validate, ship the diff with doc updates in `references/scoring/advisor-scorer.md` and the feature catalog.\n233: \n234: **Q: How does the advisor stay safe to call from hooks?**\n235: \n236: A: Responses redact raw prompt content. Attribution is per-lane only. Every runtime hook and the Python compatibility shim follow the same redaction contract, so telemetry stays safe at any call volume.\n237: \n238: **Q: What is the relationship to system-spec-kit?**\n239: \n240: A: Memory, spec folders and continuity stay in `system-spec-kit`. The advisor depends on it for the shared embeddings stack and the IPC bridge. All other code is isolated.\n241: \n242: **Q: Where are the runtime hooks documented?**\n243: \n244: A: `hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md` covers the prompt-time hook contract across every runtime (Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin, Pi) and the OpenCode plugin. The advisor's own handler, the Pi extension and the CLI fallback live under `hooks/`. Claude, Codex, Cursor and Devin enter through thin shims in `system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/<runtime>/`, and the OpenCode plugin is `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js`.\n245: \n246: ---\n247: \n248: ## 7. VERIFICATION\n249: \n250: | Check | How to run it |\n251: |---|---|\n252: | README structure | `python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md --type readme` reports zero issues |\n253: | TypeScript build | `npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run typecheck && npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build` exits 0 |\n254: | Playbook | Run the manual testing playbook scenarios under `manual-testing-playbook/` in a live session |\n255: | Validation battery | `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_validate --json '{\"confirmHeavyRun\":true}' --format json` reports within the dated bounded-delta gate in [`validation-baselines.md`](./references/scoring/validation-baselines.md) |\n256: \n257: ---\n258: \n259: ## 8. RELATED DOCUMENTS\n260: \n261: | Document | Purpose |\n262: |---|---|\n263: | [`SKILL.md`](./SKILL.md) | Runtime routing instructions and invariants |\n264: | [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) | Machine router: intent map, resource map and loading levels |\n265: | [`ARCHITECTURE.md`](./ARCHITECTURE.md) | System design, CLI front door, data flow and database layout |\n266: | [`INSTALL-GUIDE.md`](./INSTALL-GUIDE.md) | Setup, runtime hooks, rollback and embedder selection |\n267: | [`references/runtime/tool-ids-reference.md`](./references/runtime/tool-ids-reference.md) | All nine tool ids with inputs and output shapes |\n268: | [`references/scoring/advisor-scorer.md`](./references/scoring/advisor-scorer.md) | Lane attribution model and fusion rules |\n269: | [`references/scoring/lane-weight-tuning.md`](./references/scoring/lane-weight-tuning.md) | Evidence requirements for lane weight changes |\n270: | [`references/scoring/validation-baselines.md`](./references/scoring/validation-baselines.md) | Validation baselines and troubleshooting |\n271: | [`references/runtime/freshness-contract.md`](./references/runtime/freshness-contract.md) | Trust-state vocabulary and caller obligations |\n272: | [`references/runtime/daemon-lease-contract.md`](./references/runtime/daemon-lease-contract.md) | Single-writer daemon lease semantics |\n273: | [`references/runtime/standalone-mcp-shape.md`](./references/runtime/standalone-mcp-shape.md) | ADR-001 standalone topology record (historical) |\n274: | [`references/graph/skill-graph-query-cookbook.md`](./references/graph/skill-graph-query-cookbook.md) | Worked examples for all query types |\n275: | [`references/graph/skill-graph-drift.md`](./references/graph/skill-graph-drift.md) | Detect and reconcile SQLite drift from source files |\n276: | [`hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md`](./hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md) | Prompt-time hook contract across runtimes |\n277: | [`feature-catalog/feature-catalog.md`](./feature-catalog/feature-catalog.md) | Current feature inventory |\n278: | [`manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md`](./manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md) | Manual validation scenario index |\n279: | [`feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/goal-opencode-plugin.md`](./feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/goal-opencode-plugin.md) | `/goal` OpenCode plugin reference |\n280: | [`changelog/`](./changelog/) | Versioned changelogs |"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794558653314,"end":1794558654235}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794558654239,"sessionID":"ses_e2bf86ad3ffe7OMdIoF41pRkUC","part":{"partID":"prt_1d407f5ce001z6sDGJIFLIypMF","sessionID":"ses_e2bf86ad3ffe7OMdIoF41pRkUC","messageID":"msg_1d407bf58001CeiGR9VWVkUmft","type":"tool","id":"call_6862c8665b5e46a2858dd64f","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md, lines 1-79\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Command Meaning Matching\"\n3: description: \"A command row can pass on meaning when the prompt's real embedding sits close to the row's own vector, so a paraphrase that shares no words with a command can still reach it.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"command meaning matching\"\n6:   - \"command row embedding\"\n7:   - \"vec_cmd table\"\n8:   - \"command meaning anchor\"\n9: version: 1.0.0.0\n10: ---\n11: \n12: # Command Meaning Matching\n13: \n14: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n15: \n16: A command row can pass on meaning when the prompt's real embedding sits close to the row's own vector, so a paraphrase that shares no words with a command can still reach it.\n17: \n18: A command row used to pass only on one of its own phrases or its slash string. A paraphrase shares none of those words, so it reached nothing. Meaning matching adds a second way in. It works only when the local embedder runs. Without it the advisor returns the phrase-only result.\n19: \n20: ---\n21: \n22: ## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n23: \n24: ### Command Vectors\n25: \n26: `derive-command-bridges.cjs` writes an `embeddingText` for each command row the TypeScript scorer serves, 45 rows in all. A metadata row leads with its `userIntent.job` sentence and adds its owned phrases. The `description` stands in only when the record states no job. A doctor row uses its router's frontmatter `description` and the route's trigger phrases. Slash command strings are stripped from the text.\n27: \n28: `skill_graph_scan` embeds that text into a separate `vec_cmd_<dim>` table. The table carries no foreign key, and the skill vector table is left untouched. A row whose text hash has not changed is skipped. A stored row that the deriver no longer generates is pruned.\n29: \n30: ### Passing on Meaning\n31: \n32: `requireCommandPhraseAnchor` in `fusion.ts` keeps a command row passing when it matched its own phrase. A row with no phrase match can still pass when the semantic lane reports a real embedder cosine of at least `COMMAND_MEANING_MIN_COSINE` (0.72). It must also lead the strongest other command row by `COMMAND_MEANING_MARGIN` (0.02). The row is then lifted to the confidence threshold and marked as a meaning pass. The word-overlap stand-in used under test reports its score differently, and it never counts.\n33: \n34: ### Eligibility\n35: \n36: A meaning pass lifts a command past the read-only confidence floor, so `commandMeaningEligible` asks the question that floor asks of a phrase match. A prompt matching `NO_EDIT_REQUEST`, such as \"no edits\", \"do not change\", \"just show\" or \"just list\", rules out every command on meaning. A read-only explainer prompt admits only a command marked `readOnly`, because running it is the answer. `readOnlyRouteAllowed` reads the same pattern, so the two gates cannot drift apart.\n37: \n38: ### Ranking\n39: \n40: `rankMeaningPassedCommandFirst` moves the best meaning-passed command to first place only when its cosine leads the top passing skill's cosine by the same 0.02. Otherwise the skill keeps its place and the command follows it.\n41: \n42: ### Measuring It\n43: \n44: `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs --real-embedding` scores the command corpus against this machine's skill graph and live embedder. It first checks that the embedder serves `nomic-embed-text` and that a `vec_cmd_*` table holds vectors, and it refuses `--write`. The committed baseline still comes from the fixture run, which has no real cosine.\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 3. SOURCE FILES\n49: \n50: ### Implementation\n51: \n52: | File | Layer | Role |\n53: |---|---|---|\n54: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs` | Script | Builds each row's `embeddingText` |\n55: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/command-bridges.generated.json` | Generated | Carries `embeddingText` per TypeScript-served row |\n56: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/embedders/schema.ts` | Shared | Names and creates the `vec_cmd_<dim>` table |\n57: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/skill-graph/skill-graph-db.ts` | Shared | Embeds, skips and prunes command vectors during a scan |\n58: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts` | Shared | Meaning pass, its two bars, eligibility and the ranking rule |\n59: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Script | The `--real-embedding` measurement |\n60: \n61: ### Validation And Tests\n62: \n63: | File | Type | Role |\n64: |---|---|---|\n65: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-meaning-anchor.vitest.ts` | Vitest | 17 cases: cosine bar, margin, stand-in refusal, eligibility and ranking rule |\n66: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-bridges-drift-guard.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Generated bridge file matches the deriver |\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 4. SOURCE METADATA\n71: \n72: - Group: Scorer fusion\n73: - Canonical catalog source: `feature-catalog.md`\n74: - Feature file path: `scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md`\n75: \n76: Related references:\n77: \n78: - [doctor-command-routing.md](./doctor-command-routing.md) - The phrase anchor that command rows pass on by default.\n79: - [five-lane-fusion.md](./five-lane-fusion.md) - The semantic lane that supplies the cosine.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md, lines 1-79\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Command Meaning Matching\"\n3: description: \"A command row can pass on meaning when the prompt's real embedding sits close to the row's own vector, so a paraphrase that shares no words with a command can still reach it.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"command meaning matching\"\n6:   - \"command row embedding\"\n7:   - \"vec_cmd table\"\n8:   - \"command meaning anchor\"\n9: version: 1.0.0.0\n10: ---\n11: \n12: # Command Meaning Matching\n13: \n14: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n15: \n16: A command row can pass on meaning when the prompt's real embedding sits close to the row's own vector, so a paraphrase that shares no words with a command can still reach it.\n17: \n18: A command row used to pass only on one of its own phrases or its slash string. A paraphrase shares none of those words, so it reached nothing. Meaning matching adds a second way in. It works only when the local embedder runs. Without it the advisor returns the phrase-only result.\n19: \n20: ---\n21: \n22: ## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n23: \n24: ### Command Vectors\n25: \n26: `derive-command-bridges.cjs` writes an `embeddingText` for each command row the TypeScript scorer serves, 45 rows in all. A metadata row leads with its `userIntent.job` sentence and adds its owned phrases. The `description` stands in only when the record states no job. A doctor row uses its router's frontmatter `description` and the route's trigger phrases. Slash command strings are stripped from the text.\n27: \n28: `skill_graph_scan` embeds that text into a separate `vec_cmd_<dim>` table. The table carries no foreign key, and the skill vector table is left untouched. A row whose text hash has not changed is skipped. A stored row that the deriver no longer generates is pruned.\n29: \n30: ### Passing on Meaning\n31: \n32: `requireCommandPhraseAnchor` in `fusion.ts` keeps a command row passing when it matched its own phrase. A row with no phrase match can still pass when the semantic lane reports a real embedder cosine of at least `COMMAND_MEANING_MIN_COSINE` (0.72). It must also lead the strongest other command row by `COMMAND_MEANING_MARGIN` (0.02). The row is then lifted to the confidence threshold and marked as a meaning pass. The word-overlap stand-in used under test reports its score differently, and it never counts.\n33: \n34: ### Eligibility\n35: \n36: A meaning pass lifts a command past the read-only confidence floor, so `commandMeaningEligible` asks the question that floor asks of a phrase match. A prompt matching `NO_EDIT_REQUEST`, such as \"no edits\", \"do not change\", \"just show\" or \"just list\", rules out every command on meaning. A read-only explainer prompt admits only a command marked `readOnly`, because running it is the answer. `readOnlyRouteAllowed` reads the same pattern, so the two gates cannot drift apart.\n37: \n38: ### Ranking\n39: \n40: `rankMeaningPassedCommandFirst` moves the best meaning-passed command to first place only when its cosine leads the top passing skill's cosine by the same 0.02. Otherwise the skill keeps its place and the command follows it.\n41: \n42: ### Measuring It\n43: \n44: `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs --real-embedding` scores the command corpus against this machine's skill graph and live embedder. It first checks that the embedder serves `nomic-embed-text` and that a `vec_cmd_*` table holds vectors, and it refuses `--write`. The committed baseline still comes from the fixture run, which has no real cosine.\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 3. SOURCE FILES\n49: \n50: ### Implementation\n51: \n52: | File | Layer | Role |\n53: |---|---|---|\n54: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs` | Script | Builds each row's `embeddingText` |\n55: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/command-bridges.generated.json` | Generated | Carries `embeddingText` per TypeScript-served row |\n56: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/embedders/schema.ts` | Shared | Names and creates the `vec_cmd_<dim>` table |\n57: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/skill-graph/skill-graph-db.ts` | Shared | Embeds, skips and prunes command vectors during a scan |\n58: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts` | Shared | Meaning pass, its two bars, eligibility and the ranking rule |\n59: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Script | The `--real-embedding` measurement |\n60: \n61: ### Validation And Tests\n62: \n63: | File | Type | Role |\n64: |---|---|---|\n65: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-meaning-anchor.vitest.ts` | Vitest | 17 cases: cosine bar, margin, stand-in refusal, eligibility and ranking rule |\n66: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-bridges-drift-guard.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Generated bridge file matches the deriver |\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 4. SOURCE METADATA\n71: \n72: - Group: Scorer fusion\n73: - Canonical catalog source: `feature-catalog.md`\n74: - Feature file path: `scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md`\n75: \n76: Related references:\n77: \n78: - [doctor-command-routing.md](./doctor-command-routing.md) - The phrase anchor that command rows pass on by default.\n79: - [five-lane-fusion.md](./five-lane-fusion.md) - The semantic lane that supplies the cosine."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794558653902,"end":1794558654239}}}}
